---
solidHeader: true
---
{% extends "layouts/base.njk" %}

{# The iframe branch is one viewport-tall embed, so the page itself must not
   scroll — otherwise the browser shows an outer scrollbar next to the embed's
   own. `viewportPage` locks the page and drops the footer (layouts/base.njk).
   The card fallback is an ordinary scrolling page and keeps both. #}
{% set viewportPage = client.id %}

{% block content %}
{# The scrolled header is secondary-700 at 82% with a backdrop blur, so
   whatever sits behind this padding shows through it. On the near-white
   secondary-50 the bar renders as a washed-out grey while the identical
   footer stays solid — so the band carries the chrome colour explicitly. #}
<div class="bg-secondary-700 pt-[var(--header-height)]">
  {% if client.id %}
  {# The embed fills the page, so the page's own heading is screen-reader
     only — every page still needs exactly one h1.

     `pageHeading` first, not the homepage section's `title`. Being invisible
     does not make it unimportant: it is this page's strongest on-page signal
     and the only text on it outside a cross-origin iframe, so it should say
     what the page is rather than repeat a section headline written to sit
     over a row of photographs. Same trap as the /gallery/ default. Falls back
     to `title`, so a client file without the key is unchanged. #}
  <h1 class="sr-only">{{ client.menus.pageHeading or client.menus.title or "Menu" }}</h1>
  <iframe
    id="menus-frame"
    src="{{ client.urls.menusFrame }}"
    class="block w-full border-0 h-[calc(100svh-var(--header-height))]"
    title="{{ client.name }} Online Menu{% if client.onlineOrdering %} and Ordering{% endif %}"
    loading="lazy"
    allow="payment"
  >
    <!-- Fallback content for browsers that don't support iframes -->
    <p class="p-8 text-center text-gray-600">
      Your browser doesn't support iframes. 
      <a href="{{ client.urls.menusFrame }}"
         target="_blank" 
         rel="noopener noreferrer"
         class="text-primary-600 hover:text-primary-700 underline">
        Click here to view our menu in a new window
      </a>
    </p>
  </iframe>
  {#- Deep links to one menu inside the embed, e.g. a `Catering` entry in
      `client.nav` pointing at `/menus/#menu-2690`.

      The menu lives inside a cross-origin iframe. A fragment on this page is
      never sent to the server and cannot be read or scrolled to across the
      origin boundary, so the only thing that reaches the embed is putting the
      fragment on the iframe's own URL — which is what this does.

      THE SHAPE IS `#menu-<scheduleId>`, and that is a fact about the embed's
      JavaScript, not about its markup. Its `smart-menu-layout-c` controller
      reads exactly one thing out of the URL:

        resolveInitialScheduleId() {
          if (this.initialMenuIdValue > 0) return this.initialMenuIdValue;
          var t = window.location.hash.match(/^#menu-(\d+)$/);   // only shape
          return t ? Number(t[1]) : null;
        }

      It then resolves `menu_schedule<id>`, takes that section's first
      `.oc-category-section` and scrolls to it clear of the header the embed
      pins to the top of itself. So the
      number is the SCHEDULE id, and the element ids in the embed's markup
      (`menu_schedule<N>` on the section, `menu<catId>` on a category) are not
      link targets — reading the markup tells you what exists, not what a URL
      can reach. See CLAUDE.md, "Menu deep links".

      The element is REPLACED rather than having its `src` reassigned. A `src`
      change that differs from the frame's current document only by fragment is
      a fragment navigation: nothing reloads, `connect()` does not run again,
      and the embed reads the hash only in `connect()`. That failure only shows
      up on the second link — the first load works — so it hides until a site
      has two nav entries pointing at this page. `cloneNode(true)` keeps the
      id, `loading`, `allow` and the no-iframe fallback.

      The no-match branch resets the frame to its base URL, for the back button
      out of `/menus/#menu-<id>`, which is a same-document traversal. Guarded to
      the one shape so no arbitrary fragment can be appended to the embed's
      URL. -#}
  <script>
    (function () {
      var frame = document.getElementById('menus-frame');
      if (!frame) return;
      var base = frame.src.split('#')[0];
      function sync() {
        var next = /^#menu-\d+$/.test(location.hash) ? base + location.hash : base;
        if (frame.src === next) return;
        var fresh = frame.cloneNode(true);
        fresh.src = next;
        frame.replaceWith(fresh);
        frame = fresh;
      }
      sync();
      window.addEventListener('hashchange', sync);
    })();
  </script>
  {% else %}
  {# No ordering ID yet (or non-restaurant client): show the menu cards from
     the homepage section instead of a broken iframe. #}
  {% include "components/menus.njk" %}
  {% endif %}
</div>
{% endblock %}
